Assassin's Creed as a franchise has a  tendency to be very serious it's a  serious game largely featuring serious  characters in serious situations up  against serious odds  this isn't untrue of Odyssey but the  underbelly of ancient Greece was  boisterous joyful and quite frankly very  silly and it's something I don't want  the series to lose when it moves into  the age of Vikings with fall holla  Odyssey is celebrated for its massive  open world and criticized for well its  massive open world being a bit too  enormous whether you love every side  quest and story mission or find yourself  spending a little bit too much time  outrunning the bounty system there isn't  a lack of things to do in Odyssey but  it's discovering just how much of  Ubisoft ancient Greece and its  inhabitants are hilarious and batshit  crazy that makes the inconsistent pacing  and long stretches of travel of such  adelies despite the nature of the core  narrative being quite literally a Greek  tragedy the ancient history buff in me  was overjoyed to see that ub had decided  to have the greeks  not take themselves too seriously in  Odyssey Cassandra or Alexios always have  equipped ready in any situation there's  rampant casual sex and wildly silly side  quests for every murderous cultist that  needs to be hunted or god forbid  transition to the present day there's a  prophecy you've made cast or Alex  accidentally fulfilled by killing a  guy's mom who you thought was a thief  and then boning his dad a specific  comedy of errors that culminates in this  instant classic of a line you killed his  mother and his partner but the prophecy  said partner would scream to the gods in  agony oh he screamed but not in agony  and that's just the tip of the absurd  iceberg for Odysseys quests which often  provide welcome hilarity riddled detours  alongside your efforts to become the  biggest and baddest thing sailing the  Aegean  one of my favorite multistage story  quests revolves around the spottin  Olympian testiclees which is a name I've  now set on the internet you find the  athlete drunken exiled and convince him  to return to Alice to compete in the  Olympics the quest line requires a bunch  of running around  but when you finally arrived at Ellis  and this is the part where I tell you  I'm gonna spoil something so you can  skip ahead if you don't want to know  what happens testiclees promptly trips  over a bowl of fruit on the dock after  trying to hug you  falls into the water and is immediately  eaten by sharks causing you to have to  compete in the Olympics instead between  boning a new person in every port Greeks  pretending to be minotaurs saying via my  mu and a frequent use of the Malacca  expletive fun light moments and those  that make you genuinely laugh out loud  and never too hard to find in Odyssey it  also makes it super easy to empathize  with the reactions of Kassel Alex who  had generally found rolling their eyes  at the madcap Greeks and saying things  like I'm gonna start stabbing until  someone tells me what's going on not  knowing whether a quest line will end in  the dramatic slaying of a mythical  creature or if it's just somebody's  harebrained scheme gone wrong  keeps the quest lines fun even when you  have to run halfway across the Greek  Isles and back to complete them while it  would be a huge shame to miss out on  these moments of comedy when Valhalla  arrives it does seem like a less natural  fit for the Viking culture of brawler's  and Raiders a far cry from the free  loving romantic Greeks though there's a  good argument to be made for peppering  the Viking setting with moments of  appropriate levity and characters that  are unpredictable and exciting  this would help vary the tone of  Valhalla beyond the serious core  narrative just as it did in Odyssey an  important factor if we're to expect a  world just as sprawling as ubisoft's  ancient Greece Valhalla's narrative  director darby McDevitt who was also the  lead writer on revelations in Black Flag  and Cobra unity told us that Valhalla  will be primarily about the struggle of  Viking people seeking a home and the  emphasis will be on the historical  context that all sounds very serious but  McDevitt did clarify that protagonist  evil is a very unique character he  explained I won't get into it too much  but there definitely a leader they have  a sense of humor I wouldn't call them  wisecracking  but they're not stone-faced I don't know  how to get into it without spoiling too  much we already know we'll have an avian  companion again in Valhalla Leila's  story in the modern day will continue  and stealth assassinations will be even  more of a focus with the return of one  hit  kills and social stealth where you can  slip into crowds these are the things  you pretty much always expect Assassin's  Creed to get right but I hope you be  soft brings a little humor and joy to  the stories being counted in Valhalla as  well  [Music]  you
 

